---
// Works out what the reported precision/recall actually mean in operation.
// Every number shown is derived arithmetically from the two measured figures
// (precision 0.64, recall 1.00) - nothing here is invented.
---

<div class="interactive">
  <p class="interactive__intro">
    Recall of 1.00 and precision of 0.64 is an unusual pairing, and it says something specific
    about how this detector behaves. It never misses a forgery. It pays for that by calling
    some genuine images fake. Drag the slider to see what that trade looks like at volume.
  </p>

  <div class="confusion">
    <label class="confusion__label" for="confusion-range">
      Forged images in the batch: <strong data-confusion-count>100</strong>
    </label>
    <input
      class="confusion__range"
      id="confusion-range"
      type="range"
      min="10"
      max="500"
      step="10"
      value="100"
      data-confusion-range
    />

    <div class="confusion__grid">
      <div class="confusion__cell confusion__cell--tp">
        <span class="confusion__num" data-confusion-tp>100</span>
        <span class="confusion__key">Forgeries caught</span>
        <small>True positives</small>
      </div>
      <div class="confusion__cell confusion__cell--fn">
        <span class="confusion__num" data-confusion-fn>0</span>
        <span class="confusion__key">Forgeries missed</span>
        <small>False negatives</small>
      </div>
      <div class="confusion__cell confusion__cell--fp">
        <span class="confusion__num" data-confusion-fp>56</span>
        <span class="confusion__key">Genuine images wrongly flagged</span>
        <small>False positives</small>
      </div>
      <div class="confusion__cell confusion__cell--review">
        <span class="confusion__num" data-confusion-review>156</span>
        <span class="confusion__key">Total sent for human review</span>
        <small>Everything the model flags</small>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="interactive__note">
    For a forensics workflow this is arguably the right way round to be wrong: nothing
    fraudulent slips through, and the cost is a reviewer clearing roughly
    <strong data-confusion-ratio>36</strong>% of the queue as false alarms. Flip the priority
    - say, auto-rejecting flagged uploads with no human in the loop - and the same model
    becomes unusable. Measured accuracy was 82.32% on validation and 81.77% on test; the
    counts above are derived from the reported precision and recall.
  </p>
</div>

<script is:inline>
  (() => {
    const range = document.querySelector("[data-confusion-range]");
    if (!range) return;

    const PRECISION = 0.64;
    const RECALL = 1.0;

    const el = {
      count: document.querySelector("[data-confusion-count]"),
      tp: document.querySelector("[data-confusion-tp]"),
      fn: document.querySelector("[data-confusion-fn]"),
      fp: document.querySelector("[data-confusion-fp]"),
      review: document.querySelector("[data-confusion-review]"),
      ratio: document.querySelector("[data-confusion-ratio]"),
    };

    const update = () => {
      const forged = Number(range.value);
      const tp = Math.round(forged * RECALL);
      const fn = forged - tp;
      // precision = tp / (tp + fp)  =>  fp = tp * (1 / precision - 1)
      const fp = Math.round(tp * (1 / PRECISION - 1));
      const review = tp + fp;

      el.count.textContent = String(forged);
      el.tp.textContent = String(tp);
      el.fn.textContent = String(fn);
      el.fp.textContent = String(fp);
      el.review.textContent = String(review);
      el.ratio.textContent = String(Math.round((fp / review) * 100));
    };

    range.addEventListener("input", update);
    update();
  })();
</script>
